      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We start by noting that we want to find the number of four-digit positive integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9. \nSince there are only two 2s and two 9s, we cannot have more than two 2s or two 9s in the four-digit integer. \nWe begin by considering all the possible combinations of the 2s and 9s in the four-digit integer. \nThere are 3 possible cases as follows: \nCase 1. Two 2s and two 9s are both in the integer: \nCase 2. One 2 and two 9s are both in the integer: \nCase 3. Two 2s and one 9 are both in the integer: \nCase 4. Both 2s and 9s are not in the four-digit integer: \nFor Case 1, we arrange the 2s and 9s in $5!$ ways, which is 120 ways. \nFor Case 2, we arrange 1 2 and 2 9s in $4!$ ways, which is 24 ways.\nFor Case 3, we arrange 0 2s and 2 9s in $3!$ ways, which is 6 ways.\nFor Case 4, there is only one possibility where there are no 2s and 9s in the integer. \nSo we add up all the cases to get $120 + 24 + 6 + 1 = \\boxed{151}$ possibilities. \nThe answer is: 151",
