    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "$\\text{(A)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 6 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 12 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 24$\nTo determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the distinct permutations of these digits.\n\n1. **Identify the unique digits**: The digits we have are 2, 2, 9, and 9. We have two unique digits (2, 2, 9, 9), but the order in which we place them matters.\n\n2. **Use the formula for permutations of distinct objects**: Since the four digits include two identical 2s and two identical 9s, we need to calculate the number of unique permutations of these four digits.\n\nThe formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\n3. **Apply the formula**:\n\n\\[\nn = 4 \\quad (\\text{total digits})\n\\]\n\\[\nn_1 = 2 \\quad (\\text{frequency of digit 2})\n\\]\n\\[\nn_2 = 2 \\quad (\\text{frequency of digit 9})\n\\]\n\nUsing these values in the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the factorials:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow substitute these values back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{2 \\times 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
